WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.380
Open the guided notebook from the course page.

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:10.581
It uses NumPy, SciPy and Matplotlib, with no paid solver or account required for the calculations.

00:00:10.581 --> 00:00:12.711
Run cells from top to bottom.

00:00:12.711 --> 00:00:16.238
Before each result, write a prediction on paper.

00:00:16.238 --> 00:00:21.896
The notebook has working demonstration cells and a separate exercise section.

00:00:21.896 --> 00:00:27.480
The answer notebook is separate so that you can attempt your own code first.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.074
Start by writing the variable order explicitly: a, b, y A, y B.

00:00:34.074 --> 00:00:38.866
Every coefficient vector and matrix column must follow that order.

00:00:38.866 --> 00:00:47.216
SciPy minimizes, so production contribution receives negative coefficients and opening costs positive coefficients.

00:00:47.216 --> 00:00:51.790
After solving, negate the minimized objective to report profit.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:58.324
Do not negate the constraints, and do not forget the fixed costs when checking the answer.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.500
SciPy uses zero for continuous variables and one for integer variables in this example.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:13.020
To make an integer variable binary, also restrict its lower and upper bounds to zero and one.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:17.086
Setting integrality alone does not make a variable binary.

00:01:17.086 --> 00:01:20.732
We keep physical upper bounds on production as well.

00:01:20.732 --> 00:01:27.112
This makes later comparisons with loose linking constraints clear and helps catch mistakes.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.030
Write each inequality in the same variable order.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.999
The labor row is two, one, zero, zero.

00:01:35.999 --> 00:01:38.969
The sales row is one, one, zero, zero.

00:01:38.969 --> 00:01:46.863
Bring linking terms to the left, giving one, zero, minus six, zero, and zero, one, zero, minus eight.

00:01:46.863 --> 00:01:50.145
Each linking row has right hand side zero.

00:01:50.145 --> 00:01:56.632
The notebook prints the matrix so you can compare it with the model before solving.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:03.058
Call milp using the coefficients, domains and linear constraints.

00:02:03.058 --> 00:02:05.405
First check the status and message.

00:02:05.405 --> 00:02:13.519
For the baseline, the solver should find production two and eight, with both opening variables one, and profit forty six.

00:02:13.519 --> 00:02:16.469
Small floating point differences are normal.

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:25.052
A result printed near zero should be judged with a numerical tolerance, but you should not round away a large feasibility error.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:31.040
Now audit the result independently of the solver status.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:37.020
Multiply the matrix by the variable vector and compare each row with its upper bound.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:43.633
Check lower and upper variable bounds, and check that binary entries are close to zero or one.

00:02:43.633 --> 00:02:48.346
Finally recompute eight a plus six b minus ten y A minus eight y B.

00:02:48.346 --> 00:02:52.708
A matching objective alone cannot detect every modeling error.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.832
Run the same model with all integrality flags set to zero.

00:02:58.832 --> 00:03:04.532
Confirm the relaxed value is about fifty two point six seven and y A is one third.

00:03:04.532 --> 00:03:06.965
Then restore the two integer flags.

00:03:06.965 --> 00:03:12.526
Finally enumerate the four opening patterns and solve a continuous L P for each.

00:03:12.526 --> 00:03:21.632
Agreement between enumeration and M I L P is an independent check of this small model, not a proof that all future code is correct.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:28.561
Change one assumption at a time, resetting all other inputs to baseline.

00:03:28.561 --> 00:03:32.814
Reducing the sales cap to eight selects only B and gives forty.

00:03:32.814 --> 00:03:37.607
Raising B opening cost to twenty selects only A and gives thirty eight.

00:03:37.607 --> 00:03:44.628
Adding A minimum production three gives both lines open, production three and six, and profit forty two.

00:03:44.628 --> 00:03:50.772
These different decisions show why sensitivity experiments must state exactly what changed.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.763
Introduce another binary decision for one optional labor capacity module.

00:03:57.763 --> 00:04:02.317
It adds four labor units and costs seven thousand dollars per week.

00:04:02.317 --> 00:04:08.775
Move minus four k into the labor constraint and add plus seven k to the minimization objective.

00:04:08.775 --> 00:04:15.981
The best purchase plan produces six and four, with both lines open, and earns forty seven after all costs.

00:04:15.981 --> 00:04:20.808
Compare with forty six without the module: the net benefit is only one.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:27.148
Separate the operating improvement from the purchase price.

00:04:27.148 --> 00:04:36.364
With the module available, the best operating profit after line opening costs is fifty four, compared with forty six without it.

00:04:36.364 --> 00:04:38.596
The gross improvement is eight.

00:04:38.596 --> 00:04:42.124
A module cost of seven leaves one of net benefit.

00:04:42.124 --> 00:04:48.820
A cost of ten is too high, so the optimized decision is not to buy, leaving profit forty six.

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:51.484
Never subtract the module cost twice.
